V+=[
X(21,'RIDER OF LION AND SWAN',LS('Lalita’s feet attended by horsewomen, a lion and a swan nearby','HAYAMEDHA','crown'),
 'Whose feet are served by the Goddess who rides a horse; worshipped through the horse sacrifice; who rides a lion; who rides a swan; slayer of demons.',
 [('hayārūḍhāsevitāṅghriḥ','<b>whose feet are served by the horse-riding goddess</b>'),('hayamedhasamarcitā','<b>worshipped by the aśvamedha</b>'),('haryakṣavāhanā','<b>whose mount is the lion</b>'),('haṃsavāhanā','<b>whose mount is the swan</b>'),('hatadānavā','<b>by whom the dānavas are slain</b>')],
 'Hayārūḍhā, the horse-rider, is Aśvārūḍhā, the goddess who commands Lalitā’s cavalry in the war with Bhaṇḍa. In the Lalitopākhyāna each of Lalitā’s generals has a named mount and a named force.',
 'Lion and swan point to two forms: Durgā, riding the lion, and Sarasvatī or Brahmāṇī, riding the swan. Lalitā includes both, fierce and serene. The aśvamedha, the great horse sacrifice of Vedic kings, was the highest royal rite; to be worshipped through it is to be the power behind kingship. The dānavas are the demon host of Bhaṇḍa.',
 'Fierce and gentle are not opposites. Know when each is needed.',
 'On a <b>lion</b> and a <b>swan</b>.'),
X(22,'SERVED BY THE SUN',LS('The sun god bowing before Lalita, a rising sun','HARIDAŚVA','sun'),
 'Who quells sins like murder; served by the Sun and others; whose breasts are high as an elephant’s temples; the beloved wife of the one who wears the elephant hide.',
 [('hatyādipāpaśamanī','<b>who removes sins such as killing</b>'),('haridaśvādisevitā','<b>served by the Sun (of green horses) and others</b>'),('hastikumbhottuṅgakucā','<b>whose breasts are lofty like an elephant’s temples</b>'),('hastikṛttipriyāṅganā','<b>beloved of the one clad in elephant hide (Śiva)</b>')],
 'Haridaśva, “whose horses are green”, is the Sun. The Lalitā tradition makes the sun and moon, the planets and the gods all her attendants.',
 'Hatyā, killing, is listed first among the great sins in Indian law books, especially brahmahatyā. A goddess who removes even this is the greatest refuge. The bodily names here follow the convention of the Lalitā Sahasranāma, which describes the Goddess from crown to feet as an object of meditation; commentators read them as signs of abundance and nourishment, not as sensual description. Hastikṛtti, elephant hide, is Śiva’s garment after he killed the elephant demon Gajāsura.',
 'No wrong is so great that turning towards the good cannot begin to heal it.',
 'Even great <b>sins</b> can be quelled.'),
X(23,'TURMERIC AND SAFFRON',LS('Lalita smeared with turmeric and kumkum, gods bowing','HĀDIVIDYĀ','gift'),
 'Smeared with turmeric, saffron and the rest; worshipped by Indra and the gods; friend of Viṣṇu; who is the Hādi vidyā; languid with the joy of the divine drink.',
 [('haridrākuṅkumādigdhā','<b>anointed with turmeric and kumkum</b>'),('haryaśvādyamarārcitā','<b>worshipped by Indra and the other gods</b>'),('harikeśasakhī','<b>friend of Harikeśa</b>'),('hādividyā','<b>the vidyā beginning with ha</b>'),('hālāmadālasā','<b>languid with divine delight</b>')],
 'Haridrā, turmeric, and kuṅkuma, saffron-red powder, are the auspicious marks of married women and of the Goddess in South India. At Varalakṣmī Vratam and in daily pūjā they are the first offerings.',
 'Hādi vidyā is a technical term: Śrīvidyā has two main traditions of its root mantra, the Kādi, associated with Kāma, and the Hādi, associated with Lopāmudrā, Agastya’s wife. To call her the Hādi vidyā is to say she is that teaching itself; we do not reproduce it. Hālā is a divine intoxicant; in Śākta language the “joy of the drink” often means the bliss of absorption. Harikeśa is read as Viṣṇu or as Śiva.',
 'Simple offerings, turmeric and saffron, carry great meaning when given with love.',
 'Anointed with <b>turmeric and kumkum</b>.'),
X(24,'ALL-KNOWING',LS('Lalita above a turning wheel of creation, preservation and dissolution','SARVAJÑĀ','wheel'),
 'Whose form is the syllable sa; the all-knowing; ruler of all; the auspiciousness of all; creator of all; sustainer of all; destroyer of all; the eternal.',
 [('sakārarūpā','<b>whose form is the syllable sa</b>'),('sarvajñā','<b>all-knowing</b>'),('sarveśī','<b>ruler of all</b>'),('sarvamaṅgalā','<b>auspiciousness of all</b>'),('sarvakartrī · bhartrī · hantrī','<b>creator, sustainer and destroyer of all</b>'),('sanātanā','<b>the eternal</b>')],
 'A new group of twenty sa-names begins, and almost all play on sarva, “all”. Sarvamaṅgalā is also a widely used name of Durgā, recited in the well-known verse beginning “sarvamaṅgalamāṅgalye”.',
 'The three functions usually divided among Brahmā, Viṣṇu and Śiva are all given to her: kartrī, bhartrī, hantrī. This is the Śākta view that the three gods are her instruments. Sanātanā, the eternal, closes the verse: what creates, sustains and dissolves is itself beyond change.',
 'What begins things, keeps them and ends them is one. Endings are part of the same care.',
 'She <b>makes, keeps and ends</b> all.'),
X(25,'THE WITNESS IN ALL',LS('Lalita at the centre of many people, each with a small light','SARVĀTMIKĀ','lamp'),
 'Faultless in all; beautiful in every limb; witness of all; the self of all; giver of all happiness; who bewitches all.',
 [('sarvānavadyā','<b>faultless in every way</b>'),('sarvāṅgasundarī','<b>beautiful in every limb</b>'),('sarvasākṣiṇī','<b>witness of all</b>'),('sarvātmikā','<b>the self of all</b>'),('sarvasaukhyadātrī','<b>giver of all happiness</b>'),('sarvavimohinī','<b>enchantress of all</b>')],
 'Most of the nine circuits (āvaraṇas) of the Śrīcakra have names beginning with sarva, such as Sarvāśāparipūraka, Sarvasaubhāgyadāyaka, Sarvarakṣākara and Sarvānandamaya. The Triśatī’s sarva-names echo that pattern.',
 'Sarvātmikā, the self of all, is the Vedāntic claim that the same consciousness lives in every being. Sarvasākṣiṇī, the witness, is that consciousness seen as the silent observer. Sarvavimohinī can sound strange: she who bewilders all. It refers to māyā, the power by which the one appears as many; the same power, the texts say, also frees.',
 'Treat each person as if the same light lives in them. The hymn says it does.',
 'The <b>self</b> of all.'),
X(26,'THE ALL-RED MOTHER',LS('Lalita glowing red, a mother’s gesture','SARVAMĀTĀ','heart'),
 'Support of all; present everywhere; free from every defect; all red; mother of all; adorned with every ornament.',
 [('sarvādhārā','<b>support of everything</b>'),('sarvagatā','<b>all-pervading</b>'),('sarvāvaguṇavarjitā','<b>free from all faults</b>'),('sarvāruṇā','<b>wholly red</b>'),('sarvamātā','<b>mother of all</b>'),('sarvabhūṣaṇabhūṣitā','<b>adorned with all ornaments</b>')],
 'Sarvāruṇā, entirely red, describes her meditation form: red garments, red flowers, red light, like the rising sun. Red in Śākta imagery is the colour of compassion and passion, of the dawn and of auspicious kuṅkuma.',
 'Sarvamātā, mother of all, sits at the heart of the verse. After so many names of power and philosophy, the hymn returns to the simplest one. The Lalitā Sahasranāma begins with “Śrīmātā”, the holy Mother, and the Triśatī keeps that note sounding. Commentators note that a mother supports, pervades and adorns her children, as the other names in the verse say.',
 'Of all the names for the divine, mother may be the easiest to understand.',
 '<b>Mother</b> of all.'),
X(27,'KĀMEŚĪ',LS('Lalita beside Kameshvara, love revived','KĀMEŚĪ','heart'),
 'The meaning of the syllable ka; destroyer of time; Kāmeśī; giver of desired things; who revives Kāma; the perfect one; whose bosom is round and firm.',
 [('kakārārthā','<b>the meaning of the syllable ka</b>'),('kālahantrī','<b>slayer of time (death)</b>'),('kāmeśī','<b>consort of Kāmeśvara</b>'),('kāmitārthadā','<b>giver of what is desired</b>'),('kāmasañjīvanī','<b>who brings Kāma back to life</b>'),('kalyā','<b>the perfect, the ready</b>')],
 'Another group of ka-names begins. The first group described her nature as ka; this one calls her its meaning. Kāmeśī and Kāmeśvara are the names of the divine pair seated at the bindu, the centre of the Śrīcakra.',
 'Kālahantrī, slayer of Kāla, means she conquers time and death; Kāla is also a name of Yama. Kāmasañjīvanī returns to the story of verse 3: Kāma, burned by Śiva, is restored by her. In Śrīvidyā this has a spiritual meaning: desire burnt by renunciation is returned, purified, as love of the divine.',
 'Desire is not the enemy. Purified, it becomes love.',
 'She <b>revives</b> love.'),
X(28,'MOON-FACED, CLOUD-HAIRED',LS('Lalita with moon-like face, dark hair like clouds, compassion flowing from her eyes','KALĀNĀTHAMUKHĪ','moon'),
 'With thighs like an elephant’s trunk; moon-faced; whose hair puts the clouds to shame; whose side-glances stream compassion; the beloved of Kapālin (Śiva).',
 [('karabhoruḥ','<b>with shapely thighs</b>'),('kalānāthamukhī','<b>whose face is like the moon</b>'),('kacajitāmbudā','<b>whose hair surpasses the clouds</b>'),('kaṭākṣasyandikaruṇā','<b>whose glances stream compassion</b>'),('kapāliprāṇanāyikā','<b>beloved of the skull-bearer (Śiva)</b>')],
 'This and the following verses form a short head-to-foot description of the Goddess, a dhyāna in names, as in the Lalitā Sahasranāma. Each feature is compared to something in nature, following the conventions of Sanskrit poetry.',
 'Kalānātha, lord of the digits, is the moon. Kaṭākṣa, the side glance, is a recurring image in Devī hymns: Śaṅkara’s Kanakadhārā Stotram asks for one side-glance of Lakṣmī, and its compassion makes gold rain. Kapālin, the skull-bearer, is Śiva as the ascetic; she is his life’s beloved, prāṇanāyikā.',
 'A kind glance costs nothing and can change a day.',
 'Her glance <b>streams compassion</b>.'),
X(29,'COMPASSION EMBODIED',LS('Lalita with a soft voice, a conch-shaped neck, hands like young leaves','KĀRUṆYAVIGRAHĀ','heart'),
 'Compassion in bodily form; the beloved; whose radiance outshines hibiscus garlands; sweet of speech; conch-necked; whose hands surpass young shoots.',
 [('kāruṇyavigrahā','<b>embodiment of compassion</b>'),('kāntā','<b>the beloved, the lovely</b>'),('kāntidhūtajapāvaliḥ','<b>whose glow outshines rows of hibiscus</b>'),('kalālāpā','<b>sweet-voiced</b>'),('kambukaṇṭhī','<b>with a conch-like neck</b>'),('karanirjitapallavā','<b>whose hands are softer than new leaves</b>')],
 'Japā, the red hibiscus, is the flower most offered to the Goddess. A hibiscus-red Lalitā is the classic image, but here her radiance is so bright it makes the hibiscus pale.',
 'Kāruṇyavigrahā, compassion with a body, is perhaps the central idea of Devī devotion: not a distant power but mercy that has taken form so it can be approached. Kambu, the conch, is the classical comparison for a beautiful neck with three graceful lines. These descriptive names helped devotees build a vivid inner picture before meditation.',
 'Compassion needs a body: hands that help, a voice that comforts.',
 '<b>Compassion</b> with a body.'),
X(30,'THE SWAN’S GAIT',LS('Lalita walking gracefully like a swan, golden ornaments shining','HAṂSAGATI','gift'),
 'Whose arms are like wish-fulfilling creepers; adorned with a musk tilaka. The meaning of the syllable ha; who walks like a swan; resplendent with golden ornaments.',
 [('kalpavallīsamabhujā','<b>whose arms are like wish-granting creepers</b>'),('kastūrītilakāñcitā','<b>adorned with a musk mark on the forehead</b>'),('hakārārthā','<b>the meaning of ha</b>'),('haṃsagatiḥ','<b>with the gait of a swan</b>'),('hāṭakābharaṇojjvalā','<b>shining with gold ornaments</b>')],
 'The ka-group closes and a new ha-group begins mid-verse. The kalpavallī, wish-granting creeper, is a cousin of the kalpavṛkṣa: her arms grant wishes as such a creeper does.',
 'Haṃsagati, the swan’s gait, is the ideal of graceful walking in Indian poetry. The haṃsa also has a spiritual sense: it is the soul, and in yoga the natural rhythm of the breath (haṃ on the out-breath, saḥ on the in-breath). Verse 32 returns to that meaning. Kastūrī, musk, was the most precious of perfumes, and a musk tilaka a mark of royalty.',
 'Grace in movement often reflects calm in the mind.',
 'She walks like a <b>swan</b>.'),
X(31,'SLAYER WITH A SHOUT',LS('Lalita with a necklace, demons scattered by a cry','HAṬHĀTKĀRA','demon'),
 'Whose breasts are lovely with necklaces; Hākinī; free of all fault; worshipped by the lords of the directions; who killed the demons with a forceful cry.',
 [('hārahārikucābhogā','<b>whose bosom is lovely with necklaces</b>'),('hākinī','<b>Hākinī, goddess of the brow centre</b>'),('halyavarjitā','<b>free of all that is blameworthy</b>'),('haritpatisamārādhyā','<b>worshipped by the guardians of the directions</b>'),('haṭhātkārahatāsurā','<b>who slew the asuras with a sudden shout</b>')],
 'Hākinī, like Lākinī in verse 14, is one of the yoginīs of the cakras; she presides over the ājñā, the centre between the eyebrows. The two names place inner yoga within the hymn.',
 'Haritpati, “lord of a direction”, means the eight guardians of space: Indra, Agni, Yama and the rest. Haṭhātkāra, a sudden forceful sound, recalls Devī hymns in which a mere roar of the Goddess scatters armies, as in the Devī Māhātmya. Halya is read as fault or as something blameworthy; she has none.',
 'Sometimes a single firm “no” scatters what a long argument could not.',
 'One <b>cry</b> scattered the demons.'),
X(32,'THE MEANING OF HAṂSA',LS('Lalita in meditation, breath flowing like a swan','HAṂSAMANTRA','light'),
 'Giver of joy; enjoyer of offerings; dispeller of the heart’s darkness; delighted by the hallīsa dance; whose form is the meaning of the haṃsa mantra.',
 [('harṣapradā','<b>giver of delight</b>'),('havirbhoktrī','<b>enjoyer of sacrificial offerings</b>'),('hārdasantamasāpahā','<b>remover of the darkness in the heart</b>'),('hallīsalāsyasantuṣṭā','<b>pleased by the hallīsa dance</b>'),('haṃsamantrārtharūpiṇī','<b>whose form is the meaning of the haṃsa mantra</b>')],
 'Hallīsa is a circle dance, the kind of dance the gopīs danced with Kṛṣṇa; lāsya is the gentle feminine dance style, opposite Śiva’s tāṇḍava. She delights in both, as verse 12 had her witness the tāṇḍava.',
 'The haṃsa mantra, “so’ham” or “haṃsa”, is said to be repeated naturally by every breath, about 21,600 times a day: “I am That”. To be its meaning is to be the identity it states. Hārda santamas, the deep darkness in the heart, is ignorance; she dispels it like dawn. Havirbhoktrī, eater of offerings, makes her the recipient of all Vedic sacrifice.',
 'Listen to your breath for a minute. Tradition says it already prays.',
 'The meaning of every <b>breath</b>.'),
X(33,'SISTER OF HARI',LS('Lalita with Vishnu beside her as brother, celestial singers praising','HARISODARĪ','gods'),
 'Free of rejecting and accepting; the joyful one; sister of Hari; praised by Hāhā, Hūhū and the celestial singers; free of loss and gain.',
 [('hānopādānanirmuktā','<b>free from giving up and taking up</b>'),('harṣiṇī','<b>the joyous</b>'),('harisodarī','<b>sister of Viṣṇu</b>'),('hāhāhūhūmukhastutyā','<b>praised by Hāhā, Hūhū and other gandharvas</b>'),('hānivṛddhivivarjitā','<b>beyond decrease and increase</b>')],
 'Harisodarī, Viṣṇu’s sister, is a common title of Pārvatī in South India: Mīnākṣī of Madurai is married to Sundareśvara by her brother Viṣṇu (Alagar), and the wedding festival re-enacts this every year.',
 'Hāna and upādāna, giving up and taking up, are the two movements of desire and aversion; she is free of both. Hāni and vṛddhi, loss and growth, likewise do not touch her. These are descriptions of a liberated state as much as of the Goddess. Hāhā and Hūhū are two famous gandharvas, heavenly musicians.',
 'Equanimity is not coldness. It is joy that does not depend on gain or loss.',
 'Beyond <b>gain and loss</b>.'),
X(34,'HEART LIKE FRESH BUTTER',LS('Lalita with a soft heart, rising and dissolving worlds','HAYYAṄGAVĪNA','heart'),
 'Whose heart is soft as fresh butter; whose garment is red as the cowherd-boys’ cloth. Named by the syllable la; worshipped as a creeper; ruler of dissolution, maintenance and origin.',
 [('hayyaṅgavīnahṛdayā','<b>whose heart is like fresh butter</b>'),('harigopāruṇāṃśukā','<b>wearing red like an indragopa insect</b>'),('lakārākhyā','<b>named by the syllable la</b>'),('latāpūjyā','<b>worshipped as a creeper</b>'),('layasthityudbhaveśvarī','<b>ruler of dissolution, preservation and creation</b>')],
 'Hayyaṅgavīna is butter churned fresh that morning: the softest thing imaginable. Her heart melts at the first warmth of devotion.',
 'Harigopa (indragopa) is a bright red velvet mite that appears after the first rains; her garment is that red. Latā, a creeper, is a Śākta symbol for woman as the form of the Goddess; latāpūjyā is read as “worshipped as the vine”, or as “worshipped in all women”. A new la-group begins with lakārākhyā. The three cosmic functions return, now in reverse order: dissolution, maintenance, origin.',
 'A soft heart is not weak. It is the first thing that answers love.',
 'Her heart melts like <b>butter</b>.'),
X(35,'BEYOND GAIN AND LOSS',LS('Lalita watching a graceful dance, quick to grant','LAGHUSIDDHIDĀ','star'),
 'Delighted by watching the lāsya dance; free from gain and loss; whose command cannot be crossed; full of loveliness; who quickly grants accomplishment.',
 [('lāsyadarśanasantuṣṭā','<b>pleased to watch the lāsya dance</b>'),('lābhālābhavivarjitā','<b>free of gain and loss</b>'),('laṅghyetarājñā','<b>whose command cannot be overstepped</b>'),('lāvaṇyaśālinī','<b>full of beauty</b>'),('laghusiddhidā','<b>who grants success easily and quickly</b>')],
 'Lāsya, the soft, graceful dance associated with Pārvatī, is the counterpart of Śiva’s tāṇḍava. In temple tradition, dances were offered to the deity as part of worship, and many Devī hymns mention her pleasure in them.',
 'Laghusiddhidā is a hopeful name: success comes “light”, easily and quickly, to her devotees. Laṅghyetarājñā, whose order is not to be crossed, balances it: her grace is easy, her law is firm. Lābhālābha, gain and non-gain, echoes the Gītā’s ideal of equanimity in success and failure (2.48).',
 'Firm rules and quick kindness can come from the same hand.',
 'Quick to <b>grant</b>.'),
X(36,'WORSHIPPED BY RĀMA',LS('Rama worshipping Lalita, a plough nearby','LAKṢMAṆĀGRAJA','gods'),
 'Shining like lac-red; worshipped by Lakṣmaṇa’s elder brother; beyond reach otherwise; easily reached by those who have devotion; who bears the plough as weapon.',
 [('lākṣārasasavarṇābhā','<b>glowing the colour of red lac</b>'),('lakṣmaṇāgrajapūjitā','<b>worshipped by Lakṣmaṇa’s elder brother (Rāma)</b>'),('labhyetarā','<b>unreachable otherwise</b>'),('labdhabhaktisulabhā','<b>easy to reach for those with devotion</b>'),('lāṅgalāyudhā','<b>whose weapon is the plough</b>')],
 'Rāma’s worship of the Goddess is told in several traditions: in the Devī Bhāgavata and other Śākta texts he worships Durgā before the war with Rāvaṇa, and in Bengal this is the root of the autumn Durgā Pūjā.',
 'Labhyetarā and labdhabhaktisulabhā are a pair: hard to reach in any other way, easy to reach by devotion. Lāṅgala, the plough, is Balarāma’s weapon; giving it to her links her with fertility and the earth, and with Vārāhī, her general, who also carries a plough.',
 'What cannot be forced can often be reached by love.',
 'Easy to reach by <b>devotion</b>.'),
X(37,'FANNED BY LAKṢMĪ AND SARASVATĪ',LS('Lakshmi and Sarasvati holding fly-whisks beside Lalita','CĀMARA','crown'),
 'Fanned by Lakṣmī and Sarasvatī with fly-whisks in their hands; worshipped by the word of modesty; the passionate; mistress of the Lakula sect.',
 [('lagnacāmarahastaśrīśāradāparivījitā','<b>fanned by Lakṣmī and Śāradā holding chowries</b>'),('lajjāpadasamārādhyā','<b>worshipped by the syllable of modesty</b>'),('lampaṭā','<b>intensely devoted, ardent</b>'),('lakuleśvarī','<b>Goddess of the Lakula (Lakulīśa) tradition</b>')],
 'The Lalitā Sahasranāma has the same image: Lakṣmī and Sarasvatī standing on either side of the Goddess with fly-whisks (sacāmara-ramā-vāṇī). To be fanned by the goddesses of wealth and learning shows her as their queen.',
 'Lakuleśvarī is read as mistress of the Lakula or Lākula tradition, an early Śaiva school associated with Lakulīśa, whose staff (lakuṭa) gave it its name. Lampaṭā usually means “greedy” or “lustful” in ordinary Sanskrit; in devotional use it means ardent, eager, as she is eager to bless. Lajjāpada, the word of modesty, again refers to the hrīṃ syllable.',
 'Wealth and learning are best as servants of something higher.',
 'Wealth and learning <b>fan</b> her.'),
X(38,'SHE WHO HAS IT ALL',LS('Lalita crowned with a glowing seed-syllable jewel','LABDHA','crown'),
 'Who has received honour; who has tasted the essence; who has gained prosperity and greatness. Who makes hrīṃ; with hrīṃ at the start; hrīṃ in the middle; with hrīṃ as crest-jewel.',
 [('labdhamānā','<b>who has received honour</b>'),('labdharasā','<b>who has tasted the essence</b>'),('labdhasampatsamunnatiḥ','<b>who has attained wealth and eminence</b>'),('hrīṃkāriṇī','<b>who creates hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārādyā · hrīṃmadhyā','<b>with hrīṃ at the start; in the middle</b>'),('hrīṃśikhāmaṇiḥ','<b>with hrīṃ as crest-jewel</b>')],
 'The la-group closes and the second group of hrīṃ names begins. The first three names start with labdha, “attained”, a word that will return in the last la-group with many more such names.',
 'Hrīṃkārādyā, hrīṃmadhyā, hrīṃśikhāmaṇi: hrīṃ at the beginning, in the middle and at the crest. Commentators read these as positions of the seed-syllable in the structure of the root mantra. We note the reading without spelling out the structure, which is taught by a teacher.',
 'Honour and prosperity are good when the essence (rasa) is tasted too.',
 'Hrīṃ as her <b>crest-jewel</b>.'),
X(39,'FLAME, MOONLIGHT, SUNLIGHT',LS('Lalita as a flame in a fire-pit, moon and sun above','AGNIŚIKHĀ','fire'),
 'The flame in the fire-pit of hrīṃ; the moonlight of the hrīṃ moon; the radiance of the hrīṃ sun; the lightning in the hrīṃ cloud.',
 [('hrīṃkārakuṇḍāgniśikhā','<b>flame in the fire-pit of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkāraśaśicandrikā','<b>moonlight of the moon that is hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārabhāskararuciḥ','<b>light of the sun that is hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārāmbhodacañcalā','<b>lightning in the cloud that is hrīṃ</b>')],
 'From here the hrīṃ names become a sequence of images: the syllable is a fire-pit, a moon, a sun, a cloud, and she is the light within each. These verses are among the most poetic in the Triśatī.',
 'Fire, moon and sun are the three lights of Indian thought, linked in Śrīvidyā with three parts of the root mantra and three zones of the Śrīcakra. Lightning adds the sudden flash of insight. In each image, the syllable is the container and she is what shines in it: the sound is the vessel, the Goddess the light.',
 'A form is only as precious as the light it carries.',
 'The <b>light</b> in every vessel.'),
X(40,'SPROUT, SWAN, PEACOCK',LS('Lalita as a sprout, a swan on a pond and a peacock in a garden','KEKINĪ','tree'),
 'The sprout of the hrīṃ bulb; devoted solely to hrīṃ; the swan on the hrīṃ pond; the peahen in the hrīṃ garden.',
 [('hrīṃkārakandāṅkurikā','<b>the sprout from the bulb of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkāraikaparāyaṇā','<b>wholly devoted to hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkāradīrghikāhaṃsī','<b>the swan on the pond of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārodyānakekinī','<b>the peahen in the garden of hrīṃ</b>')],
 'The images turn from light to living things. A bulb and its sprout, a pond and its swan, a garden and its peacock: the syllable is the setting, the Goddess the life in it.',
 'Kanda is a bulb or root, the hidden part of a plant; aṅkura, the sprout that emerges. In mantra-yoga, the root is the syllable and the sprout is the realisation it produces. The swan on a pond returns to the haṃsa of verse 32. Kekinī, a peahen, dancing in a garden is a delightful image of joy in a cultivated place.',
 'What we tend, grows. A practice is a garden.',
 'The life in the <b>garden</b> of the seed.'),
]
